  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nStill quiet across the region today with an upper level ridge \naxis pushing through with surface high pressure over the \nIA/IL/MO border area. Southerly winds on the back side of the \nhigh were starting to pick up a bit, with a few gusts around 20 \nmph in northeast NE. Despite the stronger southerly flow, \ntemperatures were similar to those of yesterday, holding steady\nin the mid 40s as of 1 PM. Should also note a shortwave trough \ncurrently over the Four Corners region with associated precip \nextending into western KS, though guidance remains in good \nagreement that this wave and precip stay to our south. Sunday \nwill be similar to today, though a few degrees warmer.\n\nThe warming trend will continue through the first part of the \nwork week with highs around 60 by Wednesday as upper level \nridging amplifies over the central CONUS and southerly low level\nflow continues to strengthen. In fact, winds look to get pretty\ngusty on Tuesday and Wednesday as a trough digs in to the \nwestern CONUS and a surface low develops over eastern WY and \nmoves into the Dakotas, leading to a tightening pressure \ngradient over the forecast area. EPS mean gusts are in the 30-40\nmph range with model soundings suggesting 40+ mph at the top of\nthe mixed layer. There may be some fire concerns for any \nunharvested fields, though with RH favored to stay in the 50s \nand 60s, these concerns are currently relatively small. By \nWednesday evening, a cold front looks to slide through the area \nand potentially bring some showers, though guidance continues to\ntrend drier with the better moisture plume being to our east. \nChances are now around 20% for much of the area.\n\nHigher precip chances will start to arrive Thursday night and \nlast into the weekend. The general idea is that another trough \nwill move into the western CONUS Thursday with surface low \npressure developing east of the Rockies. Increasing warm air and\nmoisture advection ahead of the low is expected to lead to at \nleast some spotty showers by late Thursday night/Friday morning.\nThe actual surface low and trough then look to start passing \nthrough Friday night into Saturday, bringing widespread showers \nand perhaps a few thunderstorms to the area (80-90% chance of \nprecip). There continues to be some decent spread in timing and \ntrack of the system which will significantly impact precip \nduration and potentially type, as there will be snow on the \nnorthwest side of the low. However, a vast majority of guidance \nkeeps it rain for us, with ensembles suggesting just a 5-10% \nchance of some snow clipping our area.",
